      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your assertion highlights genuine concerns people have today regarding data privacy. While it's undeniable that some companies may have the technological capability and financial motivations to leverage personal data for psychological profiling, the ethical and legal frameworks are indeed put in place to prevent such practices without consent. It is possible for technology to be used in ways not originally intended, providing a significant incentive for companies to act transparently. Biometrics and their potential applications in fitness trackers are clearly defined by product advertisements and user agreements that outline the intended use. Consumers seeking reasonable transparency and ethical conduct from such companies can strengthen accountability through informed consent, questioning, and expecting clear answers from manufacturers. If unease persists, these anxieties can drive improvements and revolutions in data privacy laws, which in turn positively influence corporate conduct.",
